          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Transfer Functions, Block Diagram Algebra, and Signal Flow Graphs",
          "content": "# Unit 2 assessment \u2014 Time responses and second-order dynamics\n\n**Requirements:** Independently show calculations and state assumptions; answers should have units. These numbers are invented teaching examples, not equipment recommendations. Suggested duration: 2\u20133 hours plus an optional computational verification.\n\n## Part A \u2014 short quantitative checks\n\n**A1.** Analyze the case `\u03c4=0.5 s`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n**A2.** Analyze the case `\u03b6=0.5, \u03c9n=4`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n**A3.** Analyze the case `\u03b6=0.6`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n**A4.** Analyze the case `pole \u22122 rad/s`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n## Part B \u2014 synthesis problems\n\n**B1. First-order transients and time constants.** A recorded output reaches 95% in 2.5 s. Estimate tau and compare it with the estimate from a noisy 63% crossing. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n**B2. Second-order damping and natural frequency.** Solve for \u03b6 when overshoot must not exceed 10%, and discuss whether raising \u03c9n alone can meet that bound. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n**B3. Higher-order modes and approximations.** Bound the extra phase lag of the 0.1 s pole at \u03c9=1, 5 and 10 rad/s and decide at what crossover it becomes significant. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n**B4. Interpreting response measurements.** Fit one time constant using the first half of a hypothetical trace; state which second-half observations would falsify your model. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n## Part C \u2014 model criticism and experimental planning\n\n**C1.** A student produces a smooth simulated plot with an error below 1% on synthetic training data and claims the controller can be installed. Identify at least four missing kinds of evidence, including one physical limit and one timing or sensing issue. Explain how these could change the outcome even without changing the algebra.\n\n**C2.** Design a safe, offline-only demonstration for one of the four lessons. The demonstration should include at least three inputs, a falsifiable hypothesis, a baseline outcome and a deliberately perturbed parameter. State how you would decide which hypothesis survived and why the experiment does not authorize real-machine deployment.\n\n**C3.** Compare two candidate models that fit a single step response equally well but have different unobserved dynamics. Propose a measurement or excitation that could distinguish them without relying on an unsafe extreme command. Discuss whether the problem is identification, sensing, control design or all three.\n\n## Instructor reference answers and marking guidance\n\n**A1 key.** For `\u03c4=0.5 s`, the compact result is **2% settling approximately 1.96 seconds**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**A2 key.** For `\u03b6=0.5, \u03c9n=4`, the compact result is **damped frequency approximately 3.464 rad/s**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**A3 key.** For `\u03b6=0.6`, the compact result is **canonical overshoot about 9.48%**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**A4 key.** For `pole \u22122 rad/s`, the compact result is **e-fold decay time 0.5 seconds**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**B series guidance.** Mark four components independently: (i) a correct relationship and explanation of variables; (ii) a quantitative or logically determined result where data suffice; (iii) a nontrivial limiting-case or alternative-parameter test; and (iv) a credible discrimination experiment. A general statement such as \u201cthe model may be inaccurate\u201d earns little without identifying an omitted mechanism. The expected examples include the unique cases from Lessons 2.1\u20132.4.\n\n**C series guidance.** Real evidence would require verified sensor scale, actual input authority, operating envelopes, repeat tests, missing-mode investigation, documented failure handling and independent oversight. The strongest student answers name the input and output at which a discrepancy would appear, not only a generic safety list. Encourage responses that reject an overly precise conclusion when the model does not identify the needed variable.\n\n## Short self-evaluation\n\nAfter submission, label each conclusion as an identity derived from the mathematical model, a numerical result for synthetic assumptions, an empirical observation, or an engineering judgment. Confusing these categories is more serious than a minor arithmetic slip. Check every angular-frequency value against its rad/s unit and every sampled quantity against the actual sample period. Where two tests support different conclusions, do not suppress the disagreement; state the evidence gap.\n"
