          "id": "sec-3-5",
          "secNumber": "3.5",
          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Time-Domain Performance of First- and Second-Order Systems",
          "content": "# Unit 3 engineering project \u2014 Reference tracking versus disturbance rejection\n\n**Research question.** Can a compact model predict a second operating condition reliably, and what do its residuals mean? This is a synthetic computation-only assignment. No energized or safety-critical tests are required.\n\n## Case data and required work\n\nFor plant P(s)=2/(s+2) and controller C=K with K in {1,2,5,10}, derive transfer paths for a unit reference, plant-input disturbance and measurement noise. Compare DC gains, time constants and initial actuator demands. Add a saturation at \u00b15 units and explain where a linear conclusion becomes unreliable.\n\n## Reproducible workflow\n\n1. Prepare a model card containing the independent variable, measured output, units, initial conditions, parameter values and restrictions. Distinguish fixed known parameters from values estimated using the training observations. Set a reproducible numerical tolerance and record tool versions if code is used.\n2. Write the parent differential equation or characteristic relationship. Derive one check by hand and one by independent numerical evaluation. Use enough precision internally to avoid arithmetic drift, but round reported results to the underlying input precision.\n3. Build a graph that includes a physically meaningful axis range, a legend, a command trace, model output and any withheld validation output. If a logarithmic frequency axis is used, never include zero frequency as a point on that axis.\n4. Investigate at least two plausible parameter alternatives. Report which output feature changes most and whether that sensitivity points to an important new measurement. If a parameter cannot be estimated uniquely, say why instead of choosing a convenient number.\n5. Document the principal discrepancy between model and validation trace. State whether the discrepancy is likely random noise, a coherent time delay, a calibration offset, unmodeled dynamics or a regime change. Propose a specific follow-up experiment capable of distinguishing at least two possibilities.\n6. Finish with a decision memo stating what the experiment establishes, what it cannot establish, and whether further evidence is needed before any real use.\n\n## Measurement and interpretation lens\n\n**Disturbance path and control authority:** Introduce a plant-input load before the controlled process, then repeat with an additive output disturbance; the two outputs need not have the same transfer path. Measure control effort and report the effect of a nonunity sensor gain. Use a consistent sign convention so \u201cpositive disturbance\u201d has an unambiguous meaning.\n\nThe baseline computation should be viewed as a model prediction, not a design certificate. The next experiment should be chosen for information gain: an extra trace is helpful only if it can distinguish competing explanations. A practical model is sometimes deliberately simpler than the physical plant; defend simplification on the basis of bandwidth, residuals, risk, and data rather than convenience. Be explicit about initial states, since two identical inputs can yield different measured trajectories when starting conditions differ.\n\n## Required submitted artifacts\n\n- A short derivation with dimensions, sign convention and limits.\n- A machine-readable table of inputs and outputs, with units in headers and an explanation of any synthetic perturbation.\n- Two labeled plots: an explanatory baseline plot and a separate uncertainty or hold-out prediction plot.\n- An audit table separating assumptions, directly measured quantities, fitted parameters and conditions outside the model.\n- One paragraph on model usefulness; one paragraph on the strongest counterexample or failure scenario.\n\n## Evaluation rubric and common mistakes\n\n**25% theory:** valid differential equation, transfer function, characteristic equation or discrete update with correct signals. **25% arithmetic:** independently checked numerical values and declared units. **20% evaluation:** withheld-case prediction, uncertainty and an honestly labeled residual plot. **15% communication:** reproducible data, readable graphs and explicit limitations. **15% safe experimental reasoning:** no substitution of classroom simulation for real commissioning or protective design.\n\nA common failure is tuning a parameter on every available point and then citing the same points as validation. Another is reporting an impressive percentage error despite a missing or nearly zero denominator. Do not hide cases with poor fit. If the model predicts instability or impossible inputs, document the finding and stop at simulation; do not seek to reproduce an unsafe condition on a real instrument.\n"
