          "content": "# Unit 3 assessment \u2014 Feedback, sensitivity and tracking\n\n**Requirements:** Independently show calculations and state assumptions; answers should have units. These numbers are invented teaching examples, not equipment recommendations. Suggested duration: 2\u20133 hours plus an optional computational verification.\n\n## Part A \u2014 short quantitative checks\n\n**A1.** Analyze the case `L(0)=9`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n**A2.** Analyze the case `L(0)=4`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n**A3.** Analyze the case `P=2/(s+2), C=4`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n**A4.** Analyze the case `maximum command 3, required equilibrium command 5`. State the relevant relationship, what physical variable is being calculated, and the result with correct dimensions. Explain one assumption required for the answer and one reason the same expression would be inappropriate for a more complicated plant.\n\n## Part B \u2014 synthesis problems\n\n**B1. Closed-loop transfer equations.** Insert a measurement-noise term n into e=r\u2212(y+n); derive the noise-to-output transfer and explain its sign. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n**B2. Sensitivity and disturbance rejection.** Draw S and T magnitude across a chosen crossover, and identify why both cannot be arbitrarily small at the same frequency. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n**B3. Steady-state error and system type.** Compare the step and ramp errors of the two given loops; identify which design objective actually motivated the integrator. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n**B4. Actuator constraints and sensor noise.** List three experiments that distinguish a tracking error from an actuator limit, a sensor offset and a genuine unstable mode. Derive or select the governing equation; include at least one dimensional or limiting-case check. Contrast the method with the previous lesson in the unit. What extra measured variable would most reduce uncertainty? State when the calculation is valid and when it is not.\n\n## Part C \u2014 model criticism and experimental planning\n\n**C1.** A student produces a smooth simulated plot with an error below 1% on synthetic training data and claims the controller can be installed. Identify at least four missing kinds of evidence, including one physical limit and one timing or sensing issue. Explain how these could change the outcome even without changing the algebra.\n\n**C2.** Design a safe, offline-only demonstration for one of the four lessons. The demonstration should include at least three inputs, a falsifiable hypothesis, a baseline outcome and a deliberately perturbed parameter. State how you would decide which hypothesis survived and why the experiment does not authorize real-machine deployment.\n\n**C3.** Compare two candidate models that fit a single step response equally well but have different unobserved dynamics. Propose a measurement or excitation that could distinguish them without relying on an unsafe extreme command. Discuss whether the problem is identification, sensing, control design or all three.\n\n## Instructor reference answers and marking guidance\n\n**A1 key.** For `L(0)=9`, the compact result is **S(0)=0.1, T(0)=0.9**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**A2 key.** For `L(0)=4`, the compact result is **unity-step tracking error 0.2**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**A3 key.** For `P=2/(s+2), C=4`, the compact result is **closed-loop reference DC gain 0.8**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**A4 key.** For `maximum command 3, required equilibrium command 5`, the compact result is **steady target infeasible**. Require the student's derivation and units, not just this answer. The explanation should note the idealized assumption\u2014linear time-invariant dynamics, zero initial state where applicable, or the explicitly chosen sign and sampling convention. An unmodeled delay, actuation limit or sensor error can invalidate a direct application.\n\n**B series guidance.** Mark four components independently: (i) a correct relationship and explanation of variables; (ii) a quantitative or logically determined result where data suffice; (iii) a nontrivial limiting-case or alternative-parameter test; and (iv) a credible discrimination experiment. A general statement such as \u201cthe model may be inaccurate\u201d earns little without identifying an omitted mechanism. The expected examples include the unique cases from Lessons 3.1\u20133.4.\n\n**C series guidance.** Real evidence would require verified sensor scale, actual input authority, operating envelopes, repeat tests, missing-mode investigation, documented failure handling and independent oversight. The strongest student answers name the input and output at which a discrepancy would appear, not only a generic safety list. Encourage responses that reject an overly precise conclusion when the model does not identify the needed variable.\n\n## Short self-evaluation\n\nAfter submission, label each conclusion as an identity derived from the mathematical model, a numerical result for synthetic assumptions, an empirical observation, or an engineering judgment. Confusing these categories is more serious than a minor arithmetic slip. Check every angular-frequency value against its rad/s unit and every sampled quantity against the actual sample period. Where two tests support different conclusions, do not suppress the disagreement; state the evidence gap.\n"
